{"id":"claim:Q01","kind":"claim","label":"Seventeen French vessels were reported at Bantry Bay."}
{"id":"claim:Q02","kind":"claim","label":"Most vessels were described as dismasted."}
{"id":"claim:Q03","kind":"claim","label":"People landing were said to have been imprisoned and sent/went to Dublin."}
{"id":"claim:Q04","kind":"claim","label":"The writer predicted the whole force must inevitably be captured."}
{"id":"claim:Q05","kind":"claim","label":"The intended landing was said to be Londonderry."}
{"id":"claim:Q06","kind":"claim","label":"The CHRONICLE annotation challenges dates, route and numbers."}
{"id":"claim:Q07","kind":"claim","label":"A later summary says the main body returned to Brest in divisions without material loss."}
{"id":"claim:Q08","kind":"claim","label":"The Peace, cargo and specie were reported condemned at a value of $25,000."}
{"id":"claim:Q09","kind":"claim","label":"The stated pretext for seizure was arrival from a British port."}
{"id":"claim:Q10","kind":"claim","label":"The printed Cayenne decree translation cites American newspaper dates."}
{"id":"claim:Q11","kind":"claim","label":"The translation authorizes seizures involving British ports and vague destinations."}
{"id":"claim:Q12","kind":"claim","label":"An editorial note alleges seven of eight cited instances were false."}
{"id":"claim:Q13","kind":"claim","label":"The clearance resolution was approved on 7 May 1794."}
{"id":"claim:Q14","kind":"claim","label":"Loaded US-owned vessels bound beyond the Cape could be cleared subject to the resolution."}
{"id":"claim:Q15","kind":"claim","label":"Owners had to provide security against unloading before reaching the Cape."}
